Chapter: Name Resolution
  cites Codex chapter General
  cites Codex chapter Console
  cites Codex chapter Files
  cites Codex chapter Process
  cites Codex chapter Text
  cites Codex chapter Characters
  cites Codex chapter Numbers
  cites Codex chapter Bitwise
  cites Codex chapter Lists
  cites Codex chapter Concurrency
  cites Codex chapter State
  cites Codex chapter Time
  cites Codex chapter Random
  cites Codex chapter Runtime
  cites Codex chapter Phase Allocator (deck-record)
  cites Codex chapter Build Settings (max-recursion-depth)

 Checks that every name used in an expression resolves to a definition,
 builtin, constructor, type, or local binding.

Section: Scope
 A scope is two lists. `names` is the chapter-level set — top-level
 defs + ctors + builtins — built once before resolving expressions
 and never mutated per-def. `locals` is the per-def set — function
 params, `let` bindings, lambda params, pattern vars. Splitting them
 prevents the big `names` list (~2000 entries in self-host) from being
 cloned on every local binding via `list-insert-at`: without the split,
 each def's first `scope-add` fell off heap-top and allocated a fresh
 16 KB copy. With locals on their own list, the main `names` is never
 touched after chapter-scope construction.

 **Locals linearity**: `scope-add-local` uses `__record-set` which is
 in-place mutation on bare metal. That means *sibling* iterations
 (sibling defs in `resolve-all-defs`, sibling arms in
 `resolve-match-arms`, sibling clauses in `resolve-handle-clauses`)
 cannot share a Scope — one sibling's additions would leak into the
 next and silently resolve misspelled names against the previous
 sibling's bindings. Each sibling-iteration site allocates a fresh
 Scope via the `Scope { names = ..., locals = ... }` constructor so
 its mutations stay isolated. The `locals` of that fresh Scope is
 either `skip-list-text-empty` (top-level defs — no outer locals) or
 `skip-list-text-clone sc.locals` (match arms / handle clauses — a
 fresh independent skip-list so subsequent `scope-add-local` on the
 fresh scope doesn't touch the outer container).

 *Sequential* accumulators (let bindings, act-stmts, lambda param
 chains) deliberately share the same Scope across siblings — each
 binding sees previous bindings, which is the intended lexical
 semantics.

  Scope = record {
   names : SkipListText,
   locals : SkipListText
  }

  empty-scope : Scope = Scope { names = skip-list-text-empty, locals = skip-list-text-empty }

  scope-has : Scope, Text -> Boolean
  scope-has (sc) (name) =
   if skip-list-text-has (sc.locals) name then True
   else skip-list-text-has (sc.names) name

  scope-add : Scope, Text -> Scope
  scope-add (sc) (name) =
   __record-set sc "names" (skip-list-text-insert (sc.names) name)

  scope-add-local : Scope, Text -> Scope
  scope-add-local (sc) (name) =
   __record-set sc "locals" (skip-list-text-insert (sc.locals) name)

